# Orthogonality as a computed footprint

## 0. What it is and what it buys

`Orth : Term → Term → Bool` — decidable, syntactic, **sound but not complete**:

    Orth x y = true  →  SepJV x y ∧ SepJA x y

Payoff chain, each link already in the model:

    Orth at the constructor
      ⇒ (U) discharged by `decide`, not carried in `Obligations`   (uniqueAdmission_of_disjoint)
      ⇒ `select`'s filter is always ∅ or a singleton
      ⇒ the union is total on ⋃ dom(eᵢ)
      ⇒ `⋁` is the correct row again — `selOptV`, `admitOptV`, `AdmSpecs`,
        `filter_AdmSpecs`, `filter_map_AdmSpecs`, `selOptV_le_AdmAny` and all six
        attribute-side twins retire, and `Adm (.union opts) = AdmAny opts` by `rfl`.

So this **deletes** union machinery rather than adding to it. Price that in before building on `selOptV`.

## 1. The target already has a name — two of them

    def SepJV (x y : Term) : Prop := ∀ v, Adm x v = true → Adm y v = false
    def SepJA (x y : Term) : Prop :=
      (∃ μx μy, μSyn x = some μx ∧ μSyn y = some μy ∧ μx ≠ μy)   -- the member-slot test
      ∨ (∀ a, AdmA x a = true → AdmA y a = false)

`SepJA`'s **first disjunct is already the read-side slot test** of the sketch below, in the
special case "the two terms write different members outright". The draft generalises it:
same member, disjoint occupancy *within* the slot.

And `Adm`/`AdmA` are exact at all 21 constructors now, so `SepJV` is *exactly* "disjoint write
domains" — no slack between the row and the codec.

**26 hand-proved instances of this rule exist** — `Discharge.lean` 14, `Exactness.lean` 6,
`Refutations.lean` 6: `sepA_literalN_disjoint`, `sepA_refine_disjoint`, `sepV_number_opaque`,
`sepJV_set_literals`, `sepA_literal_heldLiteral`, `not_sepJV_array_literals`, … Each is one cell
of the table below, proved by hand. **That count is the argument for the function.**

## 2. The quantifier shape, which is what makes it a reducer

**Orthogonality is existential in the node.** One separating node certifies the whole pair:

> if every value in `dom(e₁)` and every value in `dom(e₂)` has a component at position `p`, and
> those two component sets are disjoint, then `dom(e₁) ∩ dom(e₂) = ∅` — a shared value would need a
> component at `p` in both sets.

So the search does **not** have to agree across the whole tree. Precisely:

    Orth x y  =  ∀ shared slot.  ∃ node inside it.  separated there

∀ over slots because a value lands in exactly one slot, so every shared slot must be closed. ∃ over
nodes because one witness per slot is enough. The `∃` is what you descend to find, and what you can
**stop descending** once you have — which is the reducer.

The load-bearing side condition is *must exist in both*. Two places it bites, both already theorems:

    array vs array      — neither excludes `[]`, no position must exist   (not_uAtCan_array_literals)
    nullable vs nullable — both admit `null`, NULL slot has no node       (not_sep_nullable_pair)

### 2.1 The state: a partition, refined

Pairwise is `k(k−1)/2` searches. The reducer does one descent: carry, per slot, a **partition of the
options still mutually ambiguous**, and accept when every class is a singleton.

    state : Slot → Partition (options)
    step  : descend one node, refine each class by what that node separates
    accept: every class a singleton
    prune : a slot whose classes are all singletons is finished — do not descend it

`O(k · depth)` instead of `O(k² · depth)`, and it reads the way the problem actually is.

**This reframes the hierarchy from earlier.** A discriminated union is the case where **one node
refines every class to a singleton at once** — that is all `tagOfV`/`tagOfA` do. Kind-separation
(`opaque`'s fixpoint) is the same thing at the top slot. So the three dischargers in the model —
`uniqueAdmission_of_kinds`, `uniqueAdmission_byTag`, `uniqueAdmission_of_sharedBase` — are one
algorithm at three depths, distinguished only by *which node does the refining*.

### 2.2 Recurse on one term, not two

Whatever the state, the per-node test must not be a pairwise case analysis: `if one is array and the
other is tuple` is a 2×2, and with 21 constructors that is n². Compute each option's occupancy at
the node independently, then compare occupancies:

    occAt : Node → Term → Occ        -- one term
    separated node x y = disjoint (occAt node x) (occAt node y)

Which is also the shape the soundness theorem wants: `uniqueAdmission_of_disjoint` takes
`SVs : List (Value → Prop)` and `SAs : List (Attr → Prop)` — one family per option, not a relation.

### 2.2b Cuts and glue: the interface at a cut is a slot map, not a tree

`Term` is an inductive **tree** — no sharing, no cycles. The DAG is in two other places: the
reducer's **memo table** (occupancy is a pure function of one subterm, so it hash-conses over shared
structure) and `src/`'s codec graph, where `lazy` is a real thunk and one codec object is referenced
from several sites. Note the memo table is available *only because* §2.2 recurses on one term — a
pairwise relation is keyed by two nodes and memoizes k² entries, not k.

**A cut is a node whose summary you can compute without descending into it**, and every cut has a
closed-form summary, which is what makes the glue cheap:

    union opts    ↦ ⋁ occ optsᵢ          -- join; the sub-certificate is NOT needed
    lazy t        ↦ least fixpoint        -- where a cycle can close (see below)
    custom _ _    ↦ ⊤                     -- constant
    held t f _    ↦ ⊤ on write            -- constant on the opaque side
    legacy t _    ↦ ⊤ on read             -- ditto
    opaq lossy    ↦ closed form from `opaqOptions`

The glue is the first line and it is the modularity result: to decide the outer union you need the
inner union's **occupancy** — ten slots — never its certificate. Constant-size boundary, so cutting
at every `union` is free and certificates compose without nesting.

**Corollary: the summary must be shallow.** If `Occ` at `L`/`M` carried a *tree* of sub-occupancies,
the lattice would have unbounded height and no fixpoint. So `Occ` is one level deep and all deeper
separation is found by the reducer's *descent*, not inside the summary. That is the same constraint
from two directions, and it is what makes the domain finite.

### 2.2c Why the fixpoint exists here and not in the semantics

`Continuity.lean` refutes a fixpoint for `union`, and the refutation is real:

> `altCodec` resolves by *unique* admission, so as the recursive results grow, admission grows, the
> filtered list grows from `[o]` to `[o, o']`, and the answer collapses from `some` to `none`. (U) is
> a negative condition on the *other* options — anti-monotone in exactly the argument a fixpoint must
> grow. … a union whose options are genuinely recursive has no fixpoint semantics at all.

**Occupancy escapes this, and the reason is the over-approximation.** Anti-monotonicity comes from
the **count** — a negative condition. Occupancy never counts; it *joins*:

    admission :  anti-monotone  →  no fixpoint  →  `lazy` under `union` unmodelled   (Continuity.lean)
    occupancy :  monotone (⋁)   →  least fixpoint exists  →  Kleene-iterates

and the domain is finite for a given graph: the `ℓ`s that can appear are the literal sets
syntactically present in it, `Occ` is shallow by §2.2b, and there are ten slots. So iteration
terminates and **the certificate layer is decidable even where the semantics does not exist.** That
is the "always decidable" claim, and the over-approximation is what buys it.

**What that is and is not good for.** In the model it certifies nothing about a recursive term: the
soundness theorem is `C → UAtAct`, and `UAtAct` needs `semsOf opts`, which needs `denote`, which a
recursive union does not have. Where it *is* the answer is `src/`: the library has genuine recursion
(`lazy` as a thunk, `codec.recursive.spec.ts`) and needs exactly this decision — "does this union
resolve?" — at construction time. So this approaches audit item 19's live gap from the other side:
not *give recursive terms semantics* (refuted), but *give recursive terms a decidable orthogonality
check* (this), which is what the library actually needs.

### 2.3 The certificate is data, and that is the structural argument

Because the witness is **one node per shared slot**, the certificate is finite and small, and it is
an inductive mirroring the term structure:

    OrthCert
      | slotsDisjoint                        -- no shared slot at all
      | atScalar  (ℓ ∩ ℓ' = ∅, by decide)
      | atLength  (disjoint length sets)
      | atPos     (i : Fin n) (OrthCert …)   -- the position, plus the sub-certificate
      | atName    (n : Name)  (OrthCert …)
      | byNames   (n required by one, refused by the other)

`decide`-able at every leaf, bounded by `Term.depth`, and — the part that matters —
**it mentions only the options' own syntax, never `denote`.**

