# 13. Model side: what is proved, and the decision it forces

## 13.1 The payoff is real, and these are its exact premises

    theorem union_row_eq_any {opts : List Term} {v : Value}
        (hone : (opts.filter (fun t => admitOptV (specOf t, Adm t) v)).length ≤ 1)
        (hsub : ∀ t ∈ opts, Adm t v = true → admitOptV (specOf t, Adm t) v = true) :
        selOptV (AdmSpecs opts) v = AdmAny opts v

Proved, axioms `[propext, Quot.sound, Double, numericHost]`. `hone` is orthogonality; `hsub` is
the premise `uniqueAdmission_of_disjoint` already takes (`hw` — a canonical pair admits its own
option). Both are in the model's existing vocabulary.

## 13.2 Correction: the criterion is on `admitOptV`, not on `Adm`

I had this wrong earlier. `select` filters by the **option semantics'** admission, and for an
object option that is `keysOk` — *names*, not contents. So `Adm`-orthogonality does **not** imply a
singleton filter. `Orth` must be stated on `admitOptV`/`admitOptA`, which is what
`UniqueAdmission` already says. Consequence: for object options, orthogonality is by **names**;
`Orth`'s M-rule (§6) is therefore the right shape and the *contents* rule is only for non-objects.

## 13.3 `hsub` is not free — and the spec says so on purpose

`specOf` ignores `rest`, so an object with an **open** rest is admitted by names and refuses maps
its own `e` accepts. Probed:

    Adm asObj oneKey                              = true    -- `object ∅ ! (codec string)`, map {b}
    admitOptV (specOf asObj, Adm asObj) oneKey    = false    -- the option refuses what it writes

So `hsub` genuinely fails. `codec-calculus.md:713` states it deliberately:

> Note what admission does _not_ read: `ρ`. An undeclared name disqualifies the option even where its
> rest codec would hold it, so an object's open part is unreachable through a union — the price of
> deciding by names is that only declared names decide, and an open object that must be an option
> should declare what it means to keep.

Two ways to discharge `hsub`, and the first needs no spec change:

- **(a) restrict `Orth` to object options with `drop`/`refuse`.** Syntactic, decidable, and exactly
  the spec's own advice ("should declare what it means to keep").
- **(b) make `specOf` read the rest** — an open rest gets no name test, so it is admitted by attempt.
  Then `hsub` is a **theorem at every term**; probed and proved:

      theorem adm_le_admitOpt : ∀ (t : Term) (v : Value),
          Adm t v = true → admitOptV (specOf' t, Adm t) v = true

  Three lines of `specOf`, and it also fixes the limitation above. **But it contradicts the
  normative paragraph, so it is JH's call.** The measurement is on record either way.

## 13.4 A congruence failure, independent of that call

`record t` and `object ∅ ! (codec t)` have **identical rows on both sides** (§5, probed), hence — since
`Adm_exact`/`AdmA_exact` are unconditional — identical `dom(e)` and `dom(p)`. But `specOf` matches
the *constructor*, so the first is admitted by attempt and the second by names:

    specOf asRec = none            admitOptV … oneKey = true
    specOf asObj = some ⟨∅, _⟩     admitOptV … oneKey = false

So **`union` is not a congruence for codec equivalence.** `codec-calculus.md:713` explains *why* `ρ`
is unread; it does not observe that this breaks the `record`/`object` derivability. Worth recording
now that the derivability is established.

## 13.4b The phase decomposition — the criterion for when an index exists

The reducer refines the partition with a separator at each step. Sorted by **what kind of thing the
separator is**:

| phase | separator | function of the *input*? | into a finite key set? | indexable? |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | slot — `Value` ctor (write) / `Member` (read) | total | yes, 10 | **yes** |
| 2 | tag `tagOfV d`/`tagOfA d`; member at a `set` | partial | yes | **yes** |
| 3 | alternative membership — the value *is* the key | partial | yes, `ℓ` | **yes** |
| 4 | opaque predicate — `refine`'s `q`, `held`'s `f`, `custom` | **no** | **no** | **no** |

> **an index exists exactly when the separator is a function of the input into a finite key set.**

`kunion` is phase 1 keyed, `dunion` phase 2, a literal-keyed union would be phase 3. Phase 4 has no
key because the separator is not a function of the input at all.

## 13.5 ~~The decision the exactness work forces~~ — RETRACTED: the deletion is unavailable

The table below is kept because the *reasoning* is right; the conclusion is not, and the reason is
phase 4.

`⋁` as the row requires **every representable union** to be orthogonal. Phase 4 is unindexable, so
either a residual general `union` exists — non-orthogonal unions stay representable, and its row must
stay the count — or phase 4 is **refused**. Refusing it is wrong:
`union(refine(string, isEmail), refine(string, isPhone))` is an ordinary schema, and the model has
`sepV_refine_disjoint`/`sepA_refine_disjoint` as *supported* dischargers for exactly it.

**So the residual union is permanent and so is `selOptV`** — by the certificate route (kernel-blocked,
§2.3) and by the index route (phase 4) alike. Two retractions, one root cause.

The consolation is real and not nothing: different constructors get different rows. Indexed unions
would get **lookup** rows, exact by construction; only the phase-4 residual keeps the count. Nothing
deletes, but most unions stop going through it.

### What each option actually buys

- **indices (`kunion`/`dunion` keyed)** — `dunion`'s 19 tag declarations, `ks.Nodup`, `htags`,
  `hdisj`, `TypedListM` become indices; `opaque` becomes an instance of a combinator rather than a
  bespoke fixpoint. Does **not** buy the `selOptV` deletion. Large: 10 tables × new constructors.
- **`Orth` as a decidable premise** — the 16 positive `sep*` theorems, ~13 of which are per-shape
  instances of one rule, collapse into one function plus one soundness theorem; the 10 `not_sep*`
  witnesses become its **test suite, already written**; (D) and (U) become `decide`-able. Touches no
  constructor.

### And the ordering argument

`Orth` is not a predicate, it is a **phase classifier**. Once it exists, running it over `src/`'s
corpus gives the **phase histogram**, and that is what decides the indices:

    mostly phase 1–2   → the indices pay for themselves
    long phase-4 tail  → the indices are additive complexity for a minority

The histogram cannot be read off before the classifier exists, and the classifier is a prerequisite
for the indices anyway (their lookup rows need `Orth` to state soundness against the residual). So:
**premise first, measure, then decide the indices.** Three refactors were killed this arc by
measuring first; here the measuring device is the thing you were going to build regardless.

## 13.5b The old table, kept for the reasoning

`Adm_exact` is now exact at **every representable term**. So if the row were `⋁` while a
non-orthogonal union remained representable, `Adm_exact` would be *false*. Therefore:

| orthogonality as | non-orthogonal unions | the row | `selOptV` et al. |
|---|---|---|---|
| a `Typed` premise | representable | must stay the **count** | **stay** |
| a **constructor field** | unrepresentable | `⋁` again, by `rfl` | **13 declarations delete** |

The two options are *not* equivalent in payoff. The `⋁` restoration — and the deletion of `selOptV`,
`admitOptV`, `AdmSpecs`, `AdmSpecs_eq`, `filter_AdmSpecs`, `filter_map_AdmSpecs`,
`selOptV_le_AdmAny` and the six attribute twins — is available **only** in the constructor-field
version. That is §2.3's `union (opts) (cert : OrthCert opts)`, and this is the argument for it.

## 13.6 Order of work

1. ~~Decide 13.3~~ **settled: (a)** — `Orth` declines object options with an open rest. No spec
   change; it is what `codec-calculus.md:713` already advises.
2. ~~Decide 13.5~~ **moot** — the constructor field is kernel-blocked (§2.3) and the deletion it was
   for is unavailable by any route (§13.5). `Orth` is a **premise**.
3. **Land the normalization lemmas** — `arrayRow`/`arrayRowA`/`recordRow` plus the domain corollaries
   via `Adm_exact`, and the §13.4 congruence note. Four lines each, probed. Worth landing alone.
4. **`Orth` as a phase classifier** on `admitOptV`/`admitOptA` (§13.2), phase by phase, discharging
   through `uniqueAdmission_of_disjoint`/`_of_labels`/`_of_kinds`/`byTag`/`_of_sharedBase`. The 10
   `not_sep*` witnesses are the test suite: `Orth` must refuse every one of them, and certifying any
   is an unsoundness.
5. **Read the phase histogram** over `src/`'s corpus, then decide the indices.

`union_row_eq_any` stays as the record of what orthogonality *would* buy if every union were
constructor-checked — which none can be. Keep it: it is the theorem that says the count row is
redundant exactly where the options are orthogonal, and it is what an indexed union's lookup row
would be proved against.

Independent of all of it: **`array_eq_tuple` / `record_eq_object` as row-equality lemmas** — four
lines each, probed — because §5's normalization cites them and §13.4 is about them.
